   "text": "Enough evidence; answering.\n\n{\"class\": \"one_plus_related\",\n \"explanation\": \"The unit detects upright, frontal faces of furry mammals: the whole eyes-plus-snout configuration of cats, foxes, red pandas, hamsters, dogs and mustelids. It also fires weakly-to-strongly on face-like frontal objects such as a classic car front (headlights and grille).\",\n \"evidence\": \"Masking the fox's face drops activation 10→3.3, while keeping only the face retains 7.9; eyes alone give 3.6 and the nose alone 1.0, so the full configuration is needed. Rotating 180° sharply reduces activation (cat 9→1.8, dog 7→1.7, fox-cat 9→3.5), which is a much larger drop than other channels show. Colour is irrelevant (car in grayscale stays at 7.3), and the car response needs the whole front (no single part exceeds 2.5).\",\n \"missing_data\": \"Responses to human faces, bird or reptile faces, and hairless or furless animal faces, to tell a mammal-face selectivity apart from a generic upright-face detector; also profile versus frontal views.\",\n \"confidence\": 0.75}",
